    overview:
      "Welcome to tort law. Contract law asks which promises you have to keep; tort law asks a different question entirely: when does the law make you pay for harm you caused someone you never made any promise to at all. It is the body of law behind almost every car accident, defective product, botched medical procedure, and ruined reputation that ends up in a courtroom without a contract anywhere in sight. Over the next forty minutes you will work through eleven ideas in the order a real negligence claim actually unfolds, and then branch out into the specific doctrines that sit alongside ordinary negligence. We start at the threshold questions every fault-based claim must clear in sequence: did the defendant owe this particular claimant a duty, did the defendant's conduct fall below the standard that duty demands, and did that breach actually and legally cause the harm. We then cover the evidentiary shortcut courts allow when direct proof of what went wrong is unavailable, before turning to the no-fault doctrines, strict liability and products liability, that dispense with the fault inquiry altogether for certain activities and goods. We close with how liability spreads across multiple wrongdoers, the defenses that can reduce or bar recovery entirely, the deliberately narrower rules for nuisance, pure economic loss, and psychiatric harm, and the separate body of doctrine protecting reputation. Keep one question running underneath everything you hear: is this a fault-based claim, where the plaintiff carries the burden of proving careless conduct, or a no-fault claim, where the defendant's care is simply irrelevant to the result.",
