        explanation:
          "Once a duty is established, breach asks whether the defendant's actual conduct fell below what that duty demanded, measured against the reasonable person of ordinary prudence under the same circumstances, an objective standard that does not bend to the particular defendant's own subjective beliefs about how careful they were being. Courts adjust the standard for certain fixed categories, a higher, professional standard of care for doctors, lawyers, and other specialists judged against their peers in the same field, a lower standard the law simply does not adjust for voluntary intoxication, and a standard adjusted for children, who are judged against a reasonable child of similar age and experience rather than a reasonable adult. Evidence of industry custom is relevant to what a reasonable person would have done but is never conclusive on its own; an entire industry can be collectively negligent, as Judge Learned Hand held in The T.J. Hooper (1932), finding a tugboat operator negligent for not carrying a radio even though it was not yet standard practice in the industry at the time. Hand's own, more analytical contribution came in United States v. Carroll Towing Co. (1947), where he framed the negligence calculus algebraically: a defendant is negligent if the burden of precaution, B, is less than the probability of harm, P, multiplied by the gravity of the harm, L, or B < PL. The formula is more often used as an explanatory framework for how courts actually reason than as something litigants plug numbers into directly.",
