        explanation:
          "Now, turn away from fault entirely. Some activities are so inherently dangerous, even when conducted with maximum care, that the law simply holds the party who undertakes them liable for resulting harm regardless of fault. The Restatement (Second) of Torts Sections 519 and 520 set out the modern American framework: an activity is abnormally dangerous if it creates a high degree of risk of serious harm that cannot be eliminated by reasonable care, is not a matter of common usage, and is inappropriate to the place where it is conducted, weighed against the activity's value to the community. Classic examples include blasting with explosives, storing large quantities of flammable or toxic materials, and certain hazardous waste disposal operations; ordinary, commonly conducted activities like driving a car, however dangerous in the aggregate, do not qualify, precisely because they are a matter of common usage the law handles through ordinary negligence instead. Strict liability also applies to the keepers of wild animals, and in most jurisdictions to domestic animals with a known dangerous propensity, the so-called 'one bite' exposure for an owner who already knows their dog has previously bitten someone. The defendant's actual level of care is simply irrelevant to liability in these cases; the only live questions are whether the activity or animal qualifies for strict liability treatment at all, and whether the plaintiff's harm was within the scope of the specific risk that made the activity abnormally dangerous in the first place.",
