          "Sweden addresses nuisance-equivalent disputes between neighbors through a combination of Jordabalken, the Land Code, whose grannelagsrätt, neighbor-law, provisions govern an olägenhet, a disturbance or inconvenience, one property's use causes another, and, for disturbances connected to an environmentally significant operation, the more specific and more frequently litigated chapter 32 of Miljöbalken, the Environmental Code, which governs liability for miljöskada, environmental damage, including noise, odor, and vibration disturbances, buller, lukt, skakningar, caused by an operation falling within its scope. Chapter 32 Miljöbalken notably imposes something close to strict liability for disturbances connected to certain categories of operation, reflecting the broader Swedish pattern, covered throughout this lecture, of using specific, targeted strict-liability statutes rather than leaving a general nuisance-style balancing test to carry the entire analytical weight the way the French or Spanish approaches covered elsewhere in this lecture series tend to.",
