      "Eleven ideas, one theme to carry forward above all others: Swedish tort law is best understood not as a single, unified litigated system but as a patchwork of specific statutory and insurance-based regimes sitting alongside a comparatively general, residual fault-based framework. Culparegeln and adekvat kausalitet supply that residual framework's basic structure, and the restrictive brottsrekvisitet rule for rent förmögenhetsskada, together with the employer-unfavorable, exculpation-free principalansvar regime, show two directions, one restrictive, one expansive, in which Swedish law departs from the comparison systems covered elsewhere in this lecture series. Strikt ansvar then steps outside the fault framework entirely for specific, named categories of dangerous activity, and the no-fault insurance systems covering traffic and medical injury go even further still, displacing adversarial litigation almost entirely for the two single largest categories of real-world personal injury, the single most distinctive feature of this whole national system. Jämkning's asymmetric treatment of property versus personal-injury contributory fault, and kränkningsersättning's criminally anchored, separately compensated recognition of dignitary harm, both reflect the same recurring instinct toward protecting personal-injury and crime victims specifically that runs throughout this lecture. Grannelagsrätt and Miljöbalken show the same targeted-statute preference applied to neighbor disputes, and förtal's criminal-code foundation, channeled through the constitutional ansvarighetssystem for published material, shows Swedish law reaching for a specific, purpose-built framework for reputation protection rather than a general civil delict category, much as France does. And preskription's considerably longer general ten-year period, itself frequently overridden by the specific timing rules of the insurance-based regimes covered earlier, is the final, fitting reminder: before answering any question about Swedish tort law, always ask first whether a specific, purpose-built regime already governs the situation, because in Sweden, more often than in any of the other four systems covered in this lecture series, the answer is usually yes.",
